<h1>Privacy</h1>

<p><strong>I don't care who you are, and I'm not tracking you.</strong></p>

<p>This is one person's website on one server. There is no analytics, no advertising, no tracking
pixel, no third-party script, and no cookie set by anyone but this server. The stylesheet and the
scripts the site uses are served from here.</p>

<h2>What is stored</h2>
<p>Nothing about you is stored unless something you do needs it.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>A session.</strong> A page that says &ldquo;saved&rdquo; after a form, or that keeps a
    half-written message, needs somewhere to put it. That is a random key in a cookie; the values
    themselves stay on this server and are deleted when the session expires &mdash; 30 minutes
    without a visit, or as long as the sign-in it belongs to if you are signed in. Signing out
    deletes them.</li>
    <li><strong>A message between pages.</strong> One page can leave a short message for another — an
    acknowledgement after a form, or the result of a job — and the page you land on takes it once and
    deletes it. It is kept on this server under the same session or account as your other data, at most
    eight messages and 64 KiB for one visitor. A message that follows the account survives signing in;
    one that follows the visit goes when the session does; and deleting the page it was addressed to
    deletes it. A message on a named topic with no page behind it stays until it is taken or it
    expires.</li>
    <li><strong>An object.</strong> A page can keep bytes — an image, a short file — under its own key or
    under yours. A page's object is public and stays until the page is deleted; one kept under your
    session, browser or account is private, served only to you, and goes when the session ends, the
    browser's key expires or the account is removed. A public object at <code>/o/{sha}</code> is cached
    for a year because its address is a hash of its bytes; a private one is never cached. Either is at
    most 8 MiB, and one visitor holds at most 64 MiB.</li>
    <li><strong>A browser preference.</strong> One page keeps a setting that belongs to this device
    rather than to you &mdash; the editor mode, so your textareas stay the way you chose them. It is a
    separate random key in a cookie, kept for up to a year and reset whenever you change it. It is
    never linked to your account, survives signing in and out, and goes when you clear this site's
    cookies.</li>
    <li><strong>A sign-in.</strong> Only if you sign in as the owner: your account name, a hash of
    your password, and a key that keeps this browser signed in for up to 30 days (or until you close
    the browser, if you did not tick &ldquo;remember me&rdquo;). Signing out deletes it, and a
    password change or a removed account ends every signed-in session.</li>
    <li><strong>A form token.</strong> Pages with a form carry a short-lived token so that another
    site cannot submit that form as you. It says nothing about you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some of what is kept is not yours at all. A page's own values, its objects and the rows it keeps
are the page's, shared by every visitor, and a page may keep a <em>secret</em> &mdash; a signing key or
a token for a service it talks to &mdash; that nothing else in the site returns, that no export carries
and that is deleted with the page. That is the page's, not a visitor's, and it says nothing about you.</p>
<p>None of these are used to build a profile. Nothing here is shared, sold or sent to anyone else,
and there is no consent banner because there is nothing to consent to.</p>

<h2>What is logged</h2>
<p>The server keeps an error log and a log of sign-in attempts, by account name. It does not record
where visitors come from, which browser they use, or who they are. It counts how often each page is
rendered, so that I can see what is slow or broken; those are numbers, not identities.</p>
<p>Database backups keep copies of what is stored, so a deleted session or sign-in can linger in an
older backup until that backup ages out.</p>

<h2>The assistant</h2>
<p>The AI assistant is available to the owner only. If you use it, your messages are sent to DeepSeek
to be answered; nothing is sent unless you use it.</p>

